  "response": {
    "data": {
      "A": "Investigate an exact sparse-obstacle algorithm rather than further scalar row-band tuning. Deduplicate and sort blockers, handle blocked endpoints, and append the destination. Compute unrestricted paths using binomial coefficients, then subtract paths passing through each earlier coordinate-wise reachable blocker. Factorials and inverse factorials through 2*n-2 are valid because this range is below MOD. This gives O(n+k²) work for k distinct blockers; the empty-grid case follows the same binomial formula. Retain B's dense algorithm when a general, overflow-safe cost estimate rejects the sparse method: the stated density bound does not make k² universally affordable. Include parsing, sorting and precomputation in measurements. Preserve correctness for n=1, duplicate blockers and boundary obstacles.",
      "B": "Own the dense serial path, starting from f6306d74a90f4bd8. Preserve its independent antidiagonal updates and ARM64 NEON modular arithmetic. Investigate flat offset-indexed blocker storage instead of vector-per-diagonal allocation, buffered integer parsing, and modest unrolling of independent SIMD groups. Measure these changes separately before combining them. Keep a portable scalar fallback and the invariant that every residue is below MOD, so each addition needs at most one subtraction. Preserve zero padding, growing/shrinking wavefront bounds, and blocker clearing before advancement. Treat 919357e09c7e47e5 as a competing complete kernel, not an automatic upgrade: its two-wavefront fusion has not beaten the simpler implementation.",
      "C": "Investigate whether coarse parallelism can improve the winning SIMD algorithm. Start from 6082a4a65d95450b's persistent-team tile-wavefront structure, but replace scalar bit-tested tile interiors with tile-local SIMD antidiagonals and compact blocker events. Explicitly inject north/west boundaries and publish south/east frontiers; the global B kernel cannot simply be pasted inside a tile without adapting its boundary assumptions. Use thread-private scratch, disjoint frontier ownership and one synchronization per tile wave. Use at most four threads and only parallel support already available in the fixed build, retaining a serial fallback. Evaluate a small number of tile granularities within the existing budget. Compare end-to-end against 8.140 ms, not merely against the slower C predecessors.",
      "G": "Use f6306d74a90f4bd8 as the incumbent. Integrate incrementally: first evaluate B's serial improvements, then A's sparse dispatcher with the best dense fallback, and finally C as an alternative dense backend where measured overhead is justified. Avoid unused factorial construction or expensive sparse preprocessing on dense fallbacks. Dispatch only through general input properties and defensible work estimates, never test identities or expected outputs. Measure every complete integration under the unchanged harness; kernel gains, preprocessing changes and threading gains are not additive. Do not mechanically merge incompatible DP layouts. Review duplicate-blocker semantics, blocked endpoints, n=1, partial tiles, sentinels, modular bounds and overflow-safe cost calculations. Keep compiler, tests, oracle, budgets and target unchanged. If no integration wins, retain the measured best standalone program.",
      "analysis": "The supplied source solves Problem D. Baseline time is 77.404 ms. B's single-antidiagonal NEON program leads at 8.140 ms, approximately 9.5× faster; its fused variant is slightly slower at 8.355 ms. A's four-row program takes 12.594 ms, C's tiled program 13.086 ms, C's chunk scan 16.305 ms, and A's row scan 23.437 ms. These rank whole programs, not isolated transformations, and provide no per-input evidence for dispatch thresholds. Prioritize the winning dense SIMD path, a complementary exact sparse algorithm, and coarse SIMD parallelism. All paths must preserve the reference computation modulo 1000000007 for every valid input."
    },
